## Context

`kotoba-lang/industry`'s registry carried a `\"5819\"` entry at
`:maturity :spec` with `:name \"Other publishing activities\"` (matching
the assigned ISIC class, independently verified via a fresh clone
before any work began) and `:repo
\"https://github.com/gftdcojp/cloud-itonami-J5819\"` (an old,
never-populated placeholder naming scheme). Confirmed no repo exists yet
at either that stale placeholder or the real
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5819` target (`gh api` 404 for both)
before any work began.

ISIC 5819 is the residual \"other publishing activities\" class: catalogs,
greeting cards, calendars, posters, advertising material, forms, and
other printed matter not elsewhere classified (i.e. not books [5811],
newspapers [5813], directories/mailing lists [5812], or software
[5820]). This is part of Wave 4 (human-facing/personal-services fleet,
ISIC sections I/P/Q/R/S/T), running in parallel with the ongoing Wave 3
(production/robotics) rollout per ADR-2607152500. Like book publishing,
\"other publishing\" touches editorial-content-decision authority and
legal-risk-clearance decisions (copyright/trademark/licensed-character
clearance for a card/calendar/poster design), so this actor is
deliberately scoped to back-office operations coordination only, with a
closed op allowlist that structurally excludes finalizing an
editorial-content decision or a legal-risk clearance decision -- those
remain exclusively human/institutional decisions, never an
auto-commit-eligible op, per Wave 4's person-facing-service safety
guardrail.

## Decision

Scaffold `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5819` as a specialty-
publishing OPERATIONS COORDINATION actor (not editorial-content-decision
authority, not a legal-risk-clearance authority), with a concrete
illustrative product line -- a specialty publisher producing greeting
cards, calendars, and posters -- documented plainly in the README.
Mirrors `cloud-itonami-isic-5811`'s verified module shape and op-set
(`store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`phase`/`operation`/`sim`,
`deps.edn`/`blueprint.edn`/README/GOVERNANCE/CODE_OF_CONDUCT/
CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY, AGPL-3.0-or-later) op-for-op, since the domain
design specified for this class uses the identical four op names as
book publishing, with fresh, specialty-publishing-specific domain logic
under the `specpubops` namespace:

1. **`specpubops.store`** -- `MemStore` (atom of EDN) behind a `Store`
   protocol; a `titles` directory keyed by `:title-id` STRING (a card/
   calendar/poster/catalog title in production), plus an append-only
   `ledger` and a `coordination-log` of committed records.
2. **`specpubops.advisor`** (\"SpecPubAdvisor\") -- deterministic mock
   advisor drafting exactly four kinds of proposal: production-record
   logging (design-status/print-run-quantity/finish-spec data),
   production-operation scheduling (design/proofing/print-run
   scheduling), distribution coordination (outbound retail/wholesale
   shipment), and content-concern flagging (copyright/trademark/
   licensed-character similarity, cultural/seasonal sensitivity, or
   suspected plagiarism of another publisher's design). Every
   proposal's `:effect` is always `:propose`; every output is censored
   downstream by the governor.
3. **`specpubops.governor`** (\"SpecPubGovernor\") -- three HARD checks,
   all permanent and un-overridable: (1) title-unverified -- the
   target title record must exist AND be independently
   `:registered?`/`:verified?` in the store before ANY proposal for it
   may commit or escalate; (2) effect-not-propose -- any `:effect`
   other than `:propose` is HARD-blocked; (3) scope-exclusion -- any
   proposal (regardless of op) whose op/summary/rationale/cites/value
   touches finalizing-an-editorial-content-decision or
   legal-risk-clearance territory is a HARD, PERMANENT block,
   unconditionally evaluated on every proposal; an op outside the
   closed four-op allowlist is folded into this same check. One
   ESCALATE (soft) gate: `:flag-content-concern` ALWAYS escalates to a
   human regardless of confidence, as does low confidence generally.
4. **`specpubops.phase`** -- Phase 0->3 staged rollout;
   `:flag-content-concern` is permanently ABSENT from every phase's
   `:auto` set (structural fact, not a rollout milestone still to
   come) -- only `:log-production-record`/`:schedule-production-
   operation`/`:coordinate-distribution` may auto-commit at phase 3
   when governor-clean.
5. **`specpubops.operation`** (\"OperationActor\") -- langgraph-clj
   StateGraph, `intake -> advise -> govern -> decide -> commit | hold |
   request-approval`, `interrupt-before #{:request-approval}` for
   human-in-the-loop sign-off, invoked exclusively via
   `langgraph.graph/run*`.
6. **`deps.edn` / `blueprint.edn` / docs** -- mirror
   `cloud-itonami-isic-5811`'s shape (`:test`/`:lint`/`:run`/`:dev`
   aliases pinned to the same `kotoba-lang/langgraph` git SHA
   `a332a770a0d2b5193f81b54483bb954fb29ef8d7`, `itonami.blueprint/*`
   metadata, scope/design/testing README sections).

### Known self-tripping bug class -- guarded against from the start

This fleet has independently rediscovered, in several sibling ISIC
actors, the same bug class: a scope-exclusion term phrased as a bare
noun (e.g. \"copyright\") accidentally matches inside the mock advisor's
own default rationale/disclaimer text for a legitimate, allowed
proposal, causing the actor to self-block on its own happy path. Every
term in `specpubops.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased as a
finalization/execution ACTION (\"legal-risk clearance\", \"finalize the
editorial-content decision\"), never a bare noun, and
`test/specpubops/advisor_test.clj` carries a dedicated regression test
(`default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`)
asserting every op's own default proposal text clears the governor's
scope-exclusion scan.

### What this actor does NOT do

Finalizing an editorial-content decision (what a card/calendar/poster
design actually depicts, whether it ships as designed), and issuing a
legal-risk clearance decision (copyright/trademark/licensed-character
clearance), both remain exclusively human/institutional decisions,
permanently, with no actor or human-approval override path -- enforced
structurally by the governor's closed op/effect allowlists and the
unconditional scope-exclusion check, not just documented.
`:flag-content-concern` is a \"surface the concern\" op only; it can
never self-clear the concern it raises, and is never a member of any
phase's `:auto` set.

